---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const published = '13 November 2026';

/**
 * Written for people searching "Mealime alternative" after the 21 October 2026
 * shutdown. Two rules this page follows, because getting either wrong would be
 * worse than not ranking at all:
 *
 * 1. Mealime shut down without an export. Nothing can recover those recipes —
 *    not Plinth, not anyone. The page says so plainly rather than letting a
 *    "switch to Plinth" button imply otherwise.
 * 2. Plinth is iPhone-only today. Mealime was on both platforms, so roughly
 *    half of anyone landing here is on Android. They're told in the first
 *    screen, not in fine print under a download button.
 */

const comparison = [
  {
    feature: 'Shopping list grouped by aisle',
    mealime: 'Yes',
    plinth: 'Yes',
    plinthWins: false,
  },
  {
    feature: 'Dietary filters and allergies',
    mealime: 'Yes',
    plinth: 'Yes',
    plinthWins: false,
  },
  {
    feature: 'Builds the week’s plan for you',
    mealime: 'Yes, automatically',
    plinth: 'No — you choose each meal',
    plinthWins: false,
  },
  {
    feature: 'Share one list with your household',
    mealime: 'No',
    plinth: 'Yes, with an invite code',
    plinthWins: true,
  },
  {
    feature: 'Save a recipe from any website',
    mealime: 'No',
    plinth: 'Yes, paste the link',
    plinthWins: true,
  },
  {
    feature: 'Calorie and protein targets',
    mealime: 'No',
    plinth: 'Yes',
    plinthWins: true,
  },
  {
    feature: 'Android',
    mealime: 'Was available',
    plinth: 'Not yet — iPhone only',
    plinthWins: false,
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Mealime alternative — Plinth"
  description="Mealime shut down on 21 October 2026 and its recipes weren't exportable. Here's an honest look at whether Plinth is a sensible replacement, and what it doesn't do."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Mealime has shut down</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {published}</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        Mealime closed on <strong>21 October 2026</strong>. Saved recipes, meal plans and
        shopping lists went with it, and there was no export. The official replacement, Meals
        Hub, lives inside the Albertsons, Safeway, Vons and Jewel-Osco grocery apps and needs an
        account with one of those stores — which rules it out entirely if you don't shop at one.
      </p>

      <p>
        So people are looking for somewhere else to go. Plinth might be it. This page is an
        honest attempt at telling you whether it is, including the parts where the answer is no.
      </p>

      <h2>First, the bad news about your recipes</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>They're gone, and nothing can bring them back.</strong> Mealime deleted personal
        data at shutdown and never offered an export, so there is no file to import — into
        Plinth or anywhere else. Any app promising to migrate your Mealime library cannot do it.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you still have a browser tab, a screenshot or an email with a recipe in it, that's
        your copy. In Plinth you can paste a link from any recipe site and it will pull out the
        ingredients and steps, which is the closest thing to a rescue route that exists.
      </p>

      <h2>Before you read further: Plinth is iPhone-only right now</h2>

      <p>
        Mealime was on both platforms. Plinth is on the App Store today, and the Android version
        is built and in testing. If you're on Android, nothing below will help you this week —
        but we'll email you the day it's out, and that's the only email you'll get.
      </p>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <a
          class="btn btn--ghost"
          href="mailto:plinthrecipes@gmail.com?subject=Plinth%20on%20Android"
        >
          Tell me when Android's ready
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>How Plinth compares</h2>
      <p>
        The rows where Mealime was better are in here too. You'd find out in a day anyway, and
        you'd be annoyed.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">&nbsp;</th>
              <th scope="col">Mealime</th>
              <th scope="col">Plinth</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              comparison.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{row.feature}</th>
                  <td>{row.mealime}</td>
                  <td class={row.plinthWins ? 'compare__win' : undefined}>{row.plinth}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>The biggest difference</h2>

      <p>
        Mealime picked your meals for you. You set your preferences and it produced a week.
        Plinth doesn't do that — you search or import recipes and drop them onto the days
        yourself. If what you loved about Mealime was not having to decide, Plinth will feel
        like more work, and you should know that before you install it.
      </p>

      <p>
        What you get in return is that the recipes are yours. Anything on the web can go in,
        the week is arranged how you want it, and the shopping list can be shared with whoever
        else does the shopping.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What Plinth actually is</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A recipe search and a place to keep what you find.</strong> Thousands of
          recipes, filtered by the cuisine, time and diet you want. Save the ones that worked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A week you arrange yourself.</strong> Breakfast, lunch, dinner and dessert on
          any day, sitting on your home screen.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A shopping list that sorts itself by aisle</strong> — and that you can share
          with the people you live with, so nobody comes home with a second bag of onions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Import from anywhere.</strong> Paste a link from any recipe site, or the
          caption from a reel, and it becomes a proper recipe you can plan and shop for.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calorie and protein targets</strong> if you want them, with recipes that fit.
          It's a general wellness feature, not medical advice.
        </li>
        <li><strong>No ads, no trackers,</strong> and no ten-paragraph story before the recipe.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What it costs</h2>

      <p>
        Search, saving, the planner and the shopping list are <strong>free</strong> and stay
        free. Importing recipes, browsing by region, the daily pick and the calorie and protein
        targets are part of Premium.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every account starts with <strong>21 days of Premium free</strong>, no card needed. After
        that it's a monthly or yearly subscription — the App Store shows the price in your own
        currency. Cancel from inside the app at any time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="wrap" style="text-align: center;">
      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 46ch; margin: 0 auto 2rem;">
        Free to start, and you'll know within a week whether it suits how you cook.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6815607292">
          Download for iPhone
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/">What else Plinth does</a>
      </div>
      <small class="btn__note">On the App Store &middot; Android in testing</small>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
